/STK11","cell_types":[{"cell_type":"Ast","gene_symbol":"STK11","gene_symbol_current":"STK11","gene_id":"ENSG00000118046","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":true,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.0326044,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0918742,"nucleus_score":2.30581,"pct_ad":0.162693,"pct_control":0.154472,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.863572,"donor_log2fc":0.0961098,"donor_lfcse":0.112496,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.00455926},{"cell_type":"Ex","gene_symbol":"STK11","gene_symbol_current":"STK11","gene_id":"ENSG00000118046","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":true,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":7.54783e-08,"nucleus_log2fc":0.086132,"nucleus_score":5.43324,"pct_ad":0.362494,"pct_control":0.360272,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.658806,"donor_log2fc":0.110718,"donor_lfcse":0.0710825,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.0212686},{"cell_type":"In","gene_symbol":"STK11","gene_symbol_current":"STK11","gene_id":"ENSG00000118046","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.83991,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0356869,"nucleus_score":0.218051,"pct_ad":0.238927,"pct_control":0.240225,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.75182,"donor_log2fc":0.0819206,"donor_lfcse":0.0613219,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.0097232},{"cell_type":"Mic","gene_symbol":"STK11","gene_symbol_current":"STK11","gene_id":"ENSG00000118046","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"lower in AD","nucleus_padj":0.665471,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.0562717,"nucleus_score":-0.556222,"pct_ad":0.129991,"pct_control":0.132373,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.968603,"donor_log2fc":-0.021619,"donor_lfcse":0.0964712,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-0.0040741},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_symbol":"STK11","gene_symbol_current":"STK11","gene_id":"ENSG00000118046","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.334878,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0794719,"nucleus_score":1.29842,"pct_ad":0.126603,"pct_control":0.118374,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.999342,"donor_log2fc":0.0496184,"donor_lfcse":0.100862,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":5.04116e-06},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_symbol":"STK11","gene_symbol_current":"STK11","gene_id":"ENSG00000118046","nucleus_tested":false,"nucleus_significant":null,"nucleus_direction":"not tested","nucleus_padj":null,"nucleus_log2fc":null,"nucleus_score":null,"pct_ad":null,"pct_control":null,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.505751,"donor_log2fc":0.18877,"donor_lfcse":0.0788918,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.0586695}],"modules":[],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000118046","entrez_id":["6794"],"uniprot":["Q15831"],"ncbi_summary":"The protein encoded by this gene is a serine/threonine kinase that regulates cell polarity and energy metabolism and functions as a tumor suppressor. Mutations in this gene have been associated with the autosomal dominant Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, as well as with skin, pancreatic, and testicular cancers. [provided by RefSeq, May 2022].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"Q15831","text":"Tumor suppressor serine/threonine-protein kinase that controls the activity of AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) family members, thereby playing a role in various processes such as cell metabolism, cell polarity, apoptosis and DNA damage response (PubMed:12805220, PubMed:14517248, PubMed:14976552, PubMed:15016379, PubMed:15733851). Acts by phosphorylating the T-loop of AMPK family proteins, thus promoting their activity: phosphorylates PRKAA1, PRKAA2, BRSK1, BRSK2, MARK1, MARK2, MARK3, MARK4, NUAK1, NUAK2, SIK1, SIK2, SIK3 and SNRK but not MELK (PubMed:12805220, PubMed:14517248, PubMed:14976552, PubMed:15016379, PubMed:15733851). Also phosphorylates non-AMPK family proteins such as STRADA, PTEN and possibly p53/TP53 (PubMed:11430832, PubMed:15987703). Acts as a key upstream regulator of AMPK by mediating phosphorylation and activation of AMPK catalytic subunits PRKAA1 and PRKAA2 and thereby regulates processes including: inhibition of signaling pathways that promote cell growth and proliferation when energy levels are low, glucose homeostasis in liver, activation of autophagy when cells undergo nutrient deprivation, and B-cell differentiation in the germinal center in response to DNA damage (PubMed:14976552). Also acts as a regulator of cellular polarity by remodeling the actin cytoskeleton (PubMed:15016379). Required for cortical neuron polarization by mediating phosphorylation and activation of BRSK1 and BRSK2, leading to axon initiation and specification (PubMed:14976552). Involved in DNA damage response: interacts with p53/TP53 and recruited to the CDKN1A/WAF1 promoter to participate in transcription activation (PubMed:11430832, PubMed:17108107, PubMed:21317932). Able to phosphorylate p53/TP53; the relevance of such result in vivo is however unclear and phosphorylation may be indirect and mediated by downstream STK11/LKB1 kinase NUAK1 (PubMed:11430832, PubMed:17108107, PubMed:21317932). Also acts as a mediator of p53/TP53-dependent apoptosis via interaction with p53/TP53: translocates to the mitochondrion during apoptosis and regulates p53/TP53-dependent apoptosis pathways (PubMed:11430832, PubMed:17108107, PubMed:21317932). Regulates UV radiation-induced DNA damage response mediated by CDKN1A (PubMed:25329316). In association with NUAK1, phosphorylates CDKN1A in response to UV radiation and contributes to its degradation which is necessary for optimal DNA repair (PubMed:25329316). [Isoform 2]: Has a role in spermiogenesis."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_04","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}